  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 250 AM CDT Fri Aug 14 2026\n\nCurrently, a broad mid/upper level ridge is centered over the \nsoutheastern quadrant of the CONUS with a trough digging into the \nPac NW. A surface trough continues to sit across the state. As the \nLLJ interacts with this stationary boundary, rain and storms have \nlead to beneficial rainfall across portions of central Kansas \novernight. An outflow boundary surging out ahead of the activity \nwill draw convection slightly further south. Current thinking is the \nstorms will stay just northwest of the I-35 corridor. This activity \nwill gradually wane throughout the early morning hours with only a \nfew lingering showers/sprinkles by daybreak. \n\nFor the rest of today, temperatures are expected to soar into the \nlower 100s once again. Depending on where exactly the surface \nboundary sets up, a few locations in central Kansas may manage to \nremain in the upper 90s for highs. Then, another round of storms is \nexpected to develop later this afternoon/early evening along the \nstationary boundary. Moderate instability along with low/mid level \nlapse rates exceeding 7 C/km will support a few severe storms with \nstrong wind gusts remaining the main threat into the early overnight \nperiod. Sub-severe rain and storms are expected to persist into the \novernight hours once again with the development of the LLJ. We'll \nrinse and repeat the forecast for Saturday with highs areawide \nexpected in the low 100s. The surface boundary looks to lift \nslightly further north during the day on Saturday, leading to \nrain/storm chances remaining primarily across central Kansas and \nnorthward on Saturday night.\n\nAs an upper level trough traverses the Canadian Prairies and the \nnorthern tier of the US, the ridge will be squashed further south. \nThe stationary boundary that has been draped across the state will \nfinally push south as a cold front on Sunday leading to more \nseasonable temperatures for Sunday into early next week. Rain and \nstorm chances will linger into Monday, but drier air is expected in \nthe wake of the front for Tuesday.\n\nBy mid week, the trough will dig into the Northeastern US and \nridging will begin to build back in over the Plains from the west. \nWe will see temperatures respond accordingly with upper 90s and \nlower 100s possible again for Tue/Wed. POPs increase towards the end \nof the forecast period as model guidance suggests multiple \nshortwaves diving across the Northern and Central Plains leading to \nincreased rain and storm chances.",
